      "reasons": [
        "The definition’s opening claims that overdetermination is a general scenario where an effect is “already fully accounted for by one set of causes,” and that adding another supposed cause would be “redundant.” The provided packet discusses redundancy specifically in the context of whole-to-parts causation when the whole is “completely determined by its parts,” but it does not provide support for the broader, general characterization used in the first sentence."
